(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609112100-the-rule-against-inventing-fixtures-does-not-cover-a-whole-oracle")
  (:adr/topic "q9-harness-store-layer")
  (:adr/title "The rule against inventing fixtures does not cover a whole oracle, only its rendered half")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang" "kotoba-lang/com-agones"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609112000-eighteen-of-twenty-harnesses-carry-the-table-layer-and-two-should-not"
                "adr-2609111100-a-generator-that-cannot-derive-a-test-should-refuse-not-invent"))
  (:adr/body
  "## The decision that had been deferred three times

The generator refused the fixture-shaped layers -- store, filter, pagination,
handler, expand -- on one argument: a generator that constructs a test input has
manufactured the very thing whose absence it exists to detect, and its arms
would go green over behaviour the production path never performs.

That argument is sound. It was applied to whole oracles, and that was the error.

## oracle-store is two things

Eight selectors. FIVE return `arr-count`; three return `str-hash` of a rendered
store.

The three hashes are exactly what the rule is about: they hash the component's
own JSON convention, so a disagreement confounds behaviour with layout, and
reproducing that convention in the Clojure side would be constructing the answer.

The five counts are not. A count has no rendering.

And the seeded row they operate on is DERIVED rather than invented: the
component's own `seeded-row` is generated from the spec table -- id prefix, one
non-required field, then the required ones. Mirroring it reads the same source.
Nothing is constructed for the test to consume; the test reads what the
component already reads.

**So the rule holds and the layer comes in, partially. The unit it applies to is
a SELECTOR, not an oracle.**

## What it buys

Every layer before this one reads declarations -- the route table, the entity
table, a fixed table of strings. This one runs the store, and the selectors are
chosen for semantics that nothing else reaches:

    retract an id that is not there   the count must not move
    put an id that is already there   it must replace, not append

Measured on `com-agones`: 5 of 5 agreeing against the component compiled to
aarch64-macos and executed through `tools/kexe_loader.c`.

## Controls

A count layer whose values are all the same proves nothing, so two:

  **the values discriminate** -- the five selectors produce three distinct
  values, 3, 2 and 0.

  **mismatched comparisons differ** -- cljc `retract existing` against native
  `seeded count` (2 vs 3), and cljc `other entity` against native `retract
  absent` (0 vs 3).

## What is still refused

`oracle-store`'s three hash selectors, and the filter, pagination, handler and
expand layers, which have not been examined selector by selector yet. The same
question applies to each of them and the answer may differ per selector rather
than per layer -- that is the shape this iteration found, and it is the way to
approach the rest.

`com-agones` is the only repository carrying this layer so far. Rolling it out
is mechanical; the decision is what was hard.")))
